Title: BricsCAD macOS automation: open -a app docPath knocks an already-running instance back to the Welcome page
Status: RESOLVED 2026-09-12. All four fixes here (docPath/existing-
  document check, document-init settle delay, startup-dialog dismissal,
  and the DETECT fix that made the fresh-per-run empty.dwg actually take
  effect) confirmed together, live, unattended:
  scripts/verify-bricscad-automation.sh phase 1 PASSED cleanly on the
  first attempt with these in place, BricsCAD healthy afterward. Full
  write-up on the parent ticket's Round 10,
  issues/closed/alfe-bricscad-automation-macos-osascript.issue — closed
  alongside it
Priority: P3
Type: bug
Depends: issues/closed/alfe-bricscad-automation-macos-osascript.issue

* What

Found live (2026-09-12) while verifying the Round 8 injection hardening
in alfe-bricscad-automation-macos-osascript.issue. The AppleScript
launcher's LAUNCHCOMMAND (built by %APPLESCRIPT-LAUNCH-COMMAND in
autolisp-front-end/source/backend-bricscad.lisp) does:

  do shell script "open -a " & quoted form of appPath & " " & quoted form of docPath

whenever a template path was discovered — unconditionally, with no check
for whether BricsCAD is already running. When BricsCAD IS already
running with a document open (a realistic, common case: alfe re-run
against the same long-lived instance during iterative testing, or a user
who already had BricsCAD open before invoking alfe), this call can knock
the main window back to its "Début" (Start/Welcome) tab instead of
reusing the existing document or opening docPath as expected — confirmed
by screenshot: the "BricsCAD Lite" window's active tab changed from
"Dessin1" to "Début" immediately after a launcher run, with no new
document tab created for docPath at all.

The console window (Historique des invites) remains visually present
after this, but typing into it then does nothing — no corruption, just
total silence, no error surfaced anywhere. This produced several
seemingly-random live-test failures during Round 8 that were eventually
traced to this, not to the keystroke-injection layer (which was
separately confirmed reliable via hand-written diagnostic scripts that
never called `open -a docPath` a second time against a running
instance).

* Why this matters now

Round 6 (2026-08-14 predecessor decision, referenced in this ticket's
own history) deliberately REMOVED a `set running to exists (processes
whose name is "bricscad")' probe from the template, reasoning that
`open -a` is idempotent for ACTIVATION and no process-name check was
needed. That reasoning holds for activation — but this round found it
does NOT extend to the DOCUMENT-OPENING side effect: `open -a app
docPath` behaves differently (and disruptively) when the app already has
a document open versus a cold start. The two concerns were conflated;
activation should stay probe-free, but opening a document should not be
attempted blindly against a session that may already have one.

* Suggested fix (not yet implemented)

  - Detect whether BricsCAD is already running (a real process check is
    warranted here specifically, unlike the activation case) and, if so,
    emit LAUNCHCOMMAND as `open -a appPath' alone (no docPath) — the
    existing "already open" branch this function already has for when no
    template is discovered at all (see %APPLESCRIPT-LAUNCH-COMMAND's
    `bundle' vs `(and bundle template-path)' branches) — rather than
    conditioning solely on "was a template discovered".
  - Verify live: with BricsCAD already running and a document open,
    confirm the Welcome page is never shown and the existing document
    (or console) stays usable.
  - Also verify the COLD-START case still works: BricsCAD not yet
    running, docPath should still be passed so a fresh launch has a
    document (and therefore a command line) to type into — this is the
    scenario %APPLESCRIPT-LAUNCH-COMMAND's docstring already documents
    as the reason docPath is passed at all ("no document, no command
    line, nowhere for the keystrokes to land").

* Notes / constraints

  - Machine-only, like its parent ticket: needs a live macOS + BricsCAD
    session to verify.
  - Do not re-litigate the keystroke-injection layer here — Round 8 of
    the parent ticket separately hardened and re-verified that machinery
    (window-focus detection, content verification, corrupted-input
    guards, Dock-collision-safe clicking, exception handling throughout);
    this ticket is specifically about the launch/document-opening step
    that runs before any of that.

* References

  - issues/closed/alfe-bricscad-automation-macos-osascript.issue (Round 8:
    how this was found, and the six-plus-two injection-layer fixes made
    in the same session)
  - autolisp-front-end/source/backend-bricscad.lisp
    - %APPLESCRIPT-LAUNCH-COMMAND (~line 1150) — builds LAUNCHCOMMAND
    - EMIT-LAUNCHER-APPLESCRIPT — calls it and fills in docPath

* Round 9 — pjb + Claude, live at the machine, 2026-09-12 (same day, continued)

** Fix implemented, refined once after a live edge case, then live-confirmed

First cut: check whether BricsCAD is already RUNNING (`exists (processes
whose name contains "bricscad")`) and skip docPath if so. Emitted
correctly, all existing + one new test green — but the FIRST live test
against a freshly-launched, running-but-no-document-yet BricsCAD (sitting
on its own Start page, exactly the state a cold launch passes through)
showed the bug this coarse check introduces: `alreadyRunning` was true
(the process existed) so docPath got skipped, leaving no document and
therefore no console to type into — the very problem docPath exists to
solve, just reached a different way.

Refined to check something more specific: does a document/console window
ALREADY EXIST — the same exclusion-by-product-name signal
FINDCONSOLEWINDOW already uses (a standard window whose name does not
contain "bricscad", which stays constant and untranslated regardless of
which document is open) — rather than mere process existence. This
correctly covers all three cases: not running at all, running with
nothing open yet, and running with a document open — docPath is passed
in the first two, skipped only in the third.

Verified live: with BricsCAD running and freshly at its Start page (no
document), the fixed launcher's LAUNCHCOMMAND correctly opened
Default-mm.dwt as a new tab (confirmed by screenshot — the tab appeared,
bold/active). This is the core fix working as intended.

** Also found: the console panel can still be CLOSED right after
LAUNCHCOMMAND opens a fresh document, and the existing F2-fallback (from
alfe-bricscad-automation-macos-osascript.issue Round 6) did not reliably
reveal it in that exact sequence, even though the identical F2 keystroke
sent by hand moments later worked immediately. Diagnosed as a timing
issue: "BricsCAD is the frontmost PROCESS" says nothing about whether a
just-opened DOCUMENT has finished initializing its UI (ribbon, console
panel) enough to act on F2. Added a `delay 1.5` immediately after the
frontmost-wait succeeds and before the console-finding logic runs — a
pragmatic fixed bound, not genuine polling for document-readiness (a
further refinement, not attempted this round).

** Full end-to-end re-verification blocked by a recurring environmental
hang, not a code issue

After the settle-delay fix, BricsCAD became unresponsive again during
the very next live run — same signature as the hang documented in
alfe-bricscad-automation-macos-osascript.issue Round 8 (process pegged
near 100% CPU, System Events itself still responsive but reports ZERO
windows for the process), but this time after only ~9 minutes of heavy
scripted interaction on a freshly-launched instance, versus hours for
the first occurrence. Two independent occurrences in one day, both
following sustained rapid synthetic clicks/keystrokes/AX queries,
suggests this BricsCAD installation/session genuinely degrades under
that kind of load rather than the first occurrence being a fluke.
Force-quit and left there rather than relaunching a third time this
round — further live confirmation of the settle-delay fix (and a full,
uninterrupted READY->request->DONE cycle through
scripts/verify-bricscad-automation.sh) is the natural next step for a
fresh session, ideally a SHORT one specifically to test whether hang
avoidance correlates with session duration/event volume as
alfe-bricscad-automation-macos-osascript.issue Round 7 already
speculated.

** Net effect

The docPath/Welcome-page bug this ticket is about is FIXED and the core
mechanism verified live (document correctly opened only when needed).
The settle-delay addition is implemented and reasoned but not yet
independently confirmed live due to the hang. Tests: alfe SBCL suite
654/654 green.

** Continued (same day): the ACTUAL acceptance script surfaced a third,
distinct root cause — a stack of startup dialogs

Ran scripts/verify-bricscad-automation.sh fresh (no BricsCAD running
beforehand) to get a clean, minimal-interaction end-to-end read. Batch
control PASSED (engine/runner healthy); automation still FAILED, status
stuck at BOOTING. Live inspection found BricsCAD sitting on its own
"BricsCAD Launcher" workspace-selection dialog — a genuinely cold launch
on this installation shows this BEFORE the Start page is even reachable.
This is exactly the "a modal or first-run dialog owns input" hypothesis
from the very first version of alfe-bricscad-automation-macos-osascript
— not eliminated after all, just not reachable in earlier rounds because
BricsCAD had already been run past it once already-running instances
were being reused throughout most of this investigation.

Confirmed live that pressing Return dismisses it (via its own
default/highlighted button, no button-name hard-coding needed) — and
revealed a SECOND dialog right behind it: "Fichier de verrouillage Dwl
trouvé" (lock-file-found), because this session's own repeated
force-quits had left the Default-mm.dwt template's .dwl lock file
behind. Also a realistic scenario in production, not just a testing
artefact: any alfe automation run that gets killed abnormally while
BricsCAD has a template open leaves exactly this behind for the NEXT
run. Return dismisses this one too, via its own default button.

Neither dialog is reachable by FINDCONSOLEWINDOW (both correctly
EXCLUDED from ever being mistaken for the console by its own
name-exclusion logic — they just never get treated as one either), and
the existing frontmost-wait only confirms BricsCAD ITSELF is frontmost,
which a dialog belonging to that same process already satisfies.

Fixed: DISMISSSTARTUPDIALOGS, called once after the settle delay and
before the main injection loop — up to 5 bounded Return presses, each
followed by a FINDCONSOLEWINDOW check that stops the loop as soon as one
succeeds. Safe when no dialog is actually present: Return then lands on
BricsCAD's own idle console prompt, a harmless no-op there, never on
some unrelated foreground application (it only runs after the
frontmost-wait above already confirmed BricsCAD has focus).

Live re-verification of the FULL fix stack (docPath check + settle delay
+ dialog dismissal together) was interrupted by a THIRD occurrence of the
recurring hang documented above and in the parent ticket's Round 8 — this
time within roughly two minutes of resumed scripted interaction. Three
occurrences in one extended session, each following a burst of automated
GUI activity regardless of what that activity specifically was (manual
dialog probing, a full launcher run, simple typed tests), makes this look
like a property of this specific BricsCAD Lite V26 installation/build
under ANY sustained rapid interaction — worth checking in a future
session whether it reproduces under rapid HUMAN interaction too (which
would point at the build/installation rather than anything
automation-specific), and whether inserting longer pauses between
automated actions measurably delays it.

Tests: 4 new checks (MACOS-LAUNCHER-APPLESCRIPT-DISMISSES-STARTUP-
DIALOGS), alfe SBCL suite 658/658 green. The dialog-dismissal mechanism
itself is implemented and logically sound, and its two constituent
Return-dismissals were each individually confirmed live (by hand,
in-sequence) — what remains is a live run of the mechanism as CALLED
FROM the actual script back-to-back with everything else, which the
recurring hang has not yet allowed to complete.

** Continued (same day, pjb): the lock-file dialog's ROOT cause was
already meant to be fixed — and wasn't, because of a second bug

pjb, on seeing the lock-file dialog: "apparently there's a locked file
it tries to load at launch time (the drawing you give it?). I guess we
must expect various different dialogs on launch." Right on both counts.
On the first: autolisp-front-end already has empty-drawing.lisp
(issues/open/empty-ressource.issue, 2026-08-30), built for EXACTLY this
— write a FRESH empty.dwg into each run's own unique, cleaned-up-after
workdir, specifically so no two runs ever share (and therefore can never
lock each other out of) the same file. It was never actually taking
effect for the automation path: DISCOVER-BRICSCAD-TEMPLATE (used by
DETECT, which runs once at backend-construction time, before any
per-invocation workdir exists) has no workdir to try the fresh-drawing
step with, so it silently fell through to the SHARED vendor template —
and DETECT cached that as the backend's TEMPLATE-PATH. Every later,
correctly workdir-aware call in START-ENGINE's automation branch checks
that cached value FIRST (as it legitimately should, so a real override
wins) and therefore never reached its own fresh-per-run discovery below
it — dead code in practice, despite its own comment already correctly
saying "a drawing of this run's own, in this run's workdir".

Net effect: every run, cold or warm, was silently sharing the ONE vendor
Default-mm.dwt template all along — precisely the failure mode
empty-ressource.issue was written to eliminate, just never actually
wired up for this path. An automation run killed abnormally while that
shared file was open (exactly what happened repeatedly this session)
leaves it .dwl-locked for every subsequent run, cold or warm.

Fixed at the root, not just reacted to: DISCOVER-BRICSCAD-TEMPLATE gained
an ALLOW-VENDOR-FALLBACK keyword (default T); DETECT now passes NIL for
it, so the backend's cached TEMPLATE-PATH is honestly NIL unless the user
actually asked for something explicit (an env var), rather than DETECT's
own non-explicit vendor-template guess masquerading as one. Every
downstream call site is UNCHANGED (still checks the cached value first,
correctly) — with DETECT no longer polluting that slot, the fresh,
workdir-aware discovery underneath is reached for real. Verified via
`alfe --print-command` (no live BricsCAD needed to check this):
docPath now resolves to "<this run's own workdir>/empty.dwg", not the
shared vendor template.

This does not replace DISMISSSTARTUPDIALOGS above — pjb's second point
stands: "various different dialogs" on a cold launch (the workspace
picker, at minimum) are a separate thing this fix does not touch, and
Return-dismissal remains the reactive safety net for whatever modal
still shows up despite the file itself no longer being shareable. The
two are complementary: this fix removes the MOST COMMON self-inflicted
cause of a stray dialog (a shared, lockable file); DISMISSSTARTUPDIALOGS
handles whatever else appears regardless.

An existing test (BRICSCAD-WINDOWS-BATCH-ARGV-HAS-SINGLE-AUTOMATION-
BEFORE-B) caught a first, wrong attempt at this fix immediately: naively
reordering the `or' at each CALL SITE (fresh-discovery-first) broke the
case where a caller legitimately sets an EXPLICIT template-path directly
(as that test does, simulating a real override) — with workdir-first
ordering, auto-discovery would have shadowed even a genuine explicit
choice, and as a side effect the test run wrote a real file to /tmp
outside its own sandbox. Reverted both call-site edits back to their
original shape; the actual bug was never the `or' order, it was DETECT
handing a non-explicit guess to slots every caller has to trust ARE
explicit when non-nil.

Tests: 3 new checks (DISCOVER-BRICSCAD-TEMPLATE-SKIPS-VENDOR-FALLBACK-
WHEN-ASKED, DETECT-DOES-NOT-CACHE-A-VENDOR-TEMPLATE-PATH, DETECT-STILL-
CAPTURES-AN-EXPLICIT-TEMPLATE-OVERRIDE), alfe SBCL suite 661/661 green.
